 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Last season, Jane Wade gave camp a new meaning with nylon tent dresses—poles and all—and technical, gorp-y separates. While Wade continued developing a robust series of characters inspired by her outdoorsy Oregon upbringing for spring 2027, she also challenged herself to refine her offering. “[It feels] more polished and mature in a young, playful way,” Wade said ahead of her runway show near Chelsea Piers.</p><p>Matching sets opened up a more ladylike avenue for the designer, though her sporty DNA remained. Just look at the opener: a sweet plaid cotton poplin tank top and matching skirt, Jane Wade-ified with an edgy double zipper and a billowing, theatrical take on the boonie hat. Same goes for the sage green jersey boatneck and coordinating knee-length skirt, finished with stitching more commonly seen on activewear.</p><p>It appears that after several consecutive seasons on the New York Fashion Week schedule, Wade isn’t feeling as much pressure to prove her worth. Take a look back at Wade’s offering this time last year, and you’ll find a collection that leant more conceptual than wearable. Now, with a well-established set of house codes, she is free to exercise more subtlety. Her signature chain mail was a standout this season: Wade played around with silhouettes, producing a swishy, mock-neck minidress that would befit a modern-day Joan of Arc and, for the finale, a slinky gown with a matching chain-mail skullcap that called to mind Josephine Baker. Of course, such a display of growth will only unlock new challenges for Wade. But so far, she’s proven that she’s up for it.</p></div>",
